1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,163
A student can know every treaty in the history book and still quarrel with a classmate for a year over a borrowed pen.

2
00:00:07,163 --> 00:00:13,244
A scientist can understand the atom perfectly and still build something that frightens the world.

3
00:00:13,244 --> 00:00:20,481
In nineteen fifty four, Bertrand Russell asked why our knowledge has grown so fast while our wisdom has not.

4
00:00:25,967 --> 00:00:32,042
Welcome to Class 12 English, Unit 15: Knowledge and Wisdom, by Bertrand Russell.

5
00:00:34,433 --> 00:00:35,541
Six parts.

6
00:00:35,541 --> 00:00:37,117
Russell and the problem.

7
00:00:37,117 --> 00:00:38,653
A sense of proportion.

8
00:00:38,653 --> 00:00:41,336
Ends, feeling and private life.

9
00:00:41,336 --> 00:00:43,523
The tyranny of the here and now.

10
00:00:43,523 --> 00:00:44,942
Can wisdom be taught?

11
00:00:44,942 --> 00:00:45,941
And why it is urgent.

12
00:00:50,300 --> 00:01:00,848
Bertrand Russell was a mathematician and philosopher, one of the founders of analytic philosophy, who wrote with Whitehead an attempt to build mathematics on pure logic.

13
00:01:00,848 --> 00:01:10,341
He was also a public figure who acted on his beliefs: a pacifist jailed in nineteen eighteen, and later a leader of the campaign against nuclear weapons.

14
00:01:10,341 --> 00:01:13,986
He won the Nobel Prize in Literature in nineteen fifty.

15
00:01:13,986 --> 00:01:21,916
He wrote this essay in nineteen fifty four, nine years after Hiroshima, in the year of a hydrogen bomb test.

16
00:01:21,916 --> 00:01:25,885
The atom was the perfect example of knowledge running ahead of wisdom.

17
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:34,159
Russell's problem: our age far surpasses all others in knowledge, with no matching increase in wisdom.

18
00:01:34,159 --> 00:01:35,383
But what is wisdom?

19
00:01:35,383 --> 00:01:37,961
Knowledge is information and skill.

20
00:01:37,961 --> 00:01:47,922
Wisdom, for Russell, is a comprehensive view of life, living in feeling as well as mind, and guarding against the harm knowledge can do.

21
00:01:47,922 --> 00:01:56,380
Your textbook's shorthand, wisdom as the practical application of knowledge, is worth learning, but in long answers use Russell's own account.

22
00:02:01,167 --> 00:02:10,661
The first factor of wisdom is a sense of proportion: the capacity to take account of all the important factors in a problem and give each its due weight.

23
00:02:10,661 --> 00:02:13,096
Specialisation makes this hard.

24
00:02:13,096 --> 00:02:25,114
A medical researcher, absorbed in his work, succeeds in lowering the infant death rate around the world, and the unforeseen result is that food supplies fall short in the most crowded regions.

25
00:02:25,114 --> 00:02:36,338
More striking still: a scientist studies the atom out of pure curiosity, and puts into the hands of powerful lunatics the means of destroying the human race.

26
00:02:36,338 --> 00:02:38,590
Russell is not against science.

27
00:02:38,590 --> 00:02:40,162
He is against narrow vision.

28
00:02:44,933 --> 00:02:47,461
But comprehensiveness is not enough.

29
00:02:47,461 --> 00:02:53,476
Wisdom also needs awareness of the ends of human life: what life is for.

30
00:02:53,476 --> 00:02:58,099
Many historians did harm because they saw facts through their own passions.

31
00:02:58,099 --> 00:03:07,474
Hegel's philosophy of history was certainly comprehensive, yet its chief lesson was that Germany had been the leading nation since four hundred A D.

32
00:03:07,474 --> 00:03:14,724
So wisdom includes feeling: men whose knowledge is wide but whose feelings are narrow lack what Russell calls wisdom.

33
00:03:15,933 --> 00:03:23,252
Wisdom is needed in private life too: in choosing ends, and in freedom from personal prejudice.

34
00:03:23,252 --> 00:03:33,448
Even a noble end can be unwise if it is impossible: those who spent their lives seeking the philosopher's stone and the elixir of life wasted them.

35
00:03:33,448 --> 00:03:38,018
Then a humble case: Mr A and Mr B hate each other.

36
00:03:38,018 --> 00:03:50,010
Ask A, and you get an appalling list of B's vices; ask B, an exactly similar list about A. Tell each what the other says, and at first the hatred grows.

37
00:03:50,010 --> 00:03:59,732
But with patience you may persuade each that the other has only the normal share of human wickedness, and then you have instilled some fragment of wisdom.

38
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:11,819
Then the essence of wisdom: emancipation, as far as possible, from the tyranny of the here and now.

39
00:04:11,819 --> 00:04:19,619
Our senses are egoistic; sight and touch start from our own bodies, and our emotions start from ourselves.

40
00:04:19,619 --> 00:04:22,275
A baby feels only its own hunger.

41
00:04:22,275 --> 00:04:29,502
As we grow, the circle widens: family, nation, all humanity, all time.

42
00:04:29,502 --> 00:04:42,846
Nobody can be completely impartial, but a continual approach towards impartiality is growth in wisdom: by knowing things remote in time and space, and by giving them their due weight in our feelings.

43
00:04:47,633 --> 00:04:49,067
Can wisdom be taught?

44
00:04:49,067 --> 00:04:53,624
Russell says yes, and that it should be an aim of education.

45
00:04:53,624 --> 00:04:59,184
We are told on Sundays to love our neighbour; on the other six days we are urged to hate.

46
00:04:59,184 --> 00:05:07,491
The parable of the Good Samaritan made a despised foreigner the neighbour, but nobody hates Samaritans any more, so we miss the point.

47
00:05:07,491 --> 00:05:15,497
To get it, Russell says, substitute Communist or anti Communist, the great hatred of the Cold War in nineteen fifty four.

48
00:05:16,700 --> 00:05:19,605
But surely it is right to hate those who do harm?

49
00:05:19,605 --> 00:05:20,868
Russell says no.

50
00:05:20,868 --> 00:05:31,402
Hatred of evil is itself a kind of bondage to evil: hate them, and you will probably become as harmful as they are, and you will not persuade them to change.

51
00:05:31,402 --> 00:05:34,722
The way out is through understanding, not hate.

52
00:05:34,722 --> 00:05:38,668
And he adds a precaution: this is not non resistance.

53
00:05:38,668 --> 00:05:44,897
Resist, but with the greatest understanding and the smallest force needed to protect what is good.

54
00:05:46,100 --> 00:05:47,742
Is wisdom weak, then?

55
00:05:47,742 --> 00:05:49,318
History says not.

56
00:05:49,318 --> 00:06:01,297
Queen Elizabeth the First and Henry the Fourth of France lived among fanatics on both sides of a religious war, stayed free of the errors of their time, and were both beneficial and effective.

57
00:06:01,297 --> 00:06:06,375
Abraham Lincoln conducted a great war without ever departing from wisdom.

58
00:06:06,375 --> 00:06:08,171
Wisdom is not weakness.

59
00:06:12,967 --> 00:06:14,153
How should it be taught?

60
00:06:14,153 --> 00:06:18,008
Give moral instruction a larger intellectual element.

61
00:06:18,008 --> 00:06:24,635
Point out the disastrous results of hatred and narrow mindedness in the course of teaching knowledge.

62
00:06:24,635 --> 00:06:30,234
Set every specialist skill inside a wider survey of human life.

63
00:06:30,234 --> 00:06:37,292
Even the best technicians should be good citizens, citizens of the world, not of a sect or nation.

64
00:06:37,292 --> 00:06:51,549
And the final argument: with every increase of knowledge and skill, wisdom becomes more necessary, because more power to realise our purposes means more capacity for evil, if our purposes are unwise.

65
00:06:51,549 --> 00:06:55,230
The world needs wisdom as it has never needed it before.

66
00:06:57,633 --> 00:06:58,404
Pause and think.

67
00:07:05,700 --> 00:07:12,258
Freeing ourselves, as far as possible, from seeing everything from our own small place and moment.

68
00:07:14,233 --> 00:07:14,668
One more.

69
00:07:22,300 --> 00:07:25,422
Wide knowledge with narrow feeling is not wisdom.

70
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:28,695
Quick recap.

71
00:07:28,695 --> 00:07:30,531
Russell in the nuclear age.

72
00:07:30,531 --> 00:07:32,563
Knowledge outrunning wisdom.

73
00:07:32,563 --> 00:07:37,302
Proportion, awareness of ends, and freedom from prejudice.

74
00:07:37,302 --> 00:07:40,375
The essence: escaping the here and now.

75
00:07:40,375 --> 00:07:43,799
And why, the more we know, the more wisdom we need.

76
00:07:47,967 --> 00:07:49,922
That was Knowledge and Wisdom.

77
00:07:49,922 --> 00:07:52,161
Next essay: Humility.

78
00:07:52,161 --> 00:07:52,725
See you there.

